  "description": "<p>The \"hasTombstone\" link header does not currently check to see if there is already a trailing slash on an identifier before adding \"/fcr:tombstone\". The result is urls like \"http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme//fcr:tombstone\". This is usually not a problem, but some clients do weird things with consecutive slash characters that cause subsequent requests to fail. See:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/rest-assured/rest-assured/issues/867\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://github.com/rest-assured/rest-assured/issues/867</a></p>\n\n<p>I haven't verified if this affects 4.7.x.</p>\n\n<p>Here is an example:</p>\n<blockquote><p>curl -vi -XPUT <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme</a> -ufedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin</p>\n\n<p>curl -vi -XDELETE <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme</a> -ufedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin</p>\n\n<p>curl -vi <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme</a> -ufedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin</p>\n<ol>\n\t<li>&lt; HTTP/1.1 410 Gone</li>\n\t<li>&lt; Link: &lt;<a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme/fcr:tombstone\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme/fcr:tombstone</a>&gt;; rel=\"hasTombstone\"</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n<p>curl -vi <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme/\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme/</a> -ufedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin</p>\n<ol>\n\t<li>&lt; HTTP/1.1 410 Gone</li>\n\t<li>&lt; Link: &lt;<a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme//fcr:tombstone\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/deleteme//fcr:tombstone</a>&gt;; rel=\"hasTombstone\"</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>",
